technology.

210
00:14:57,020 --> 00:15:01,131
[George] Exactly, exactly. And there's
a lot of wisdom there, you know,

211
00:15:01,151 --> 00:15:05,918
because, like we always tell our
younger people when they join the firm,

212
00:15:05,938 --> 00:15:11,099
experience is expensive and good
judgment only comes from bad judgment.

213
00:15:11,119 --> 00:15:15,717
And you need a lot of years
to accumulate that wisdom.

214
00:15:15,737 --> 00:15:21,690
So if you think that you're going to outrun
that without a lot of reps and painful

215
00:15:21,710 --> 00:15:24,080
bad experiences, then
you're kidding yourself.

216
00:15:24,100 --> 00:15:30,058
[Jordan] Yeah, for sure. And it seems like,
in general, the real edge is going to be with

217
00:15:30,078 --> 00:15:36,330
these senior partner lawyers who bother to
pick up the technology and figure it out,

218
00:15:36,350 --> 00:15:40,758
because they're going to be
able to magnify their judgment.

219
00:15:40,778 --> 00:15:46,883
Again, to your point about wisdom and gray
hair, it just takes experience and reps

220
00:15:46,903 --> 00:15:53,343
to really earn judgment. If they can
figure out how to use this, then the rest

221
00:15:53,363 --> 00:15:55,480
of us are cooked.

222
00:15:55,500 --> 00:15:59,583
[George] Yeah, exactly. You know, it's interesting
you say that, because I read this a while

223
00:15:59,603 --> 00:16:05,205
ago, but it was talking about a
lot of the Silicon Valley startups,

224
00:16:05,225 --> 00:16:10,330
modern tech companies with Gen
X and now millennial managers.

225
00:16:10,350 --> 00:16:14,876
There's a lot of age
discrimination in those cultures.

226
00:16:14,896 --> 00:16:21,241
And a lot of boomers got wiped out of
the workforce in the financial crisis,

227
00:16:21,261 --> 00:16:26,863
and they couldn't come back in.
One thing I'm looking to do for us,

228
00:16:26,883 --> 00:16:31,366
and I'll just say the quiet part out loud,
is figure out ways that we can use this

229
00:16:31,386 --> 00:16:36,412
technology to work with those boomers
and give them another 10 years of life.

230
00:16:36,432 --> 00:16:42,002
Because if you can hitch your wagon to
somebody with that much experience and give

231
00:16:42,022 --> 00:16:46,783
them a way to help you in your
business, that's an enormous advantage.

232
00:16:46,803 --> 00:16:52,171
But just YOLOing off thinking that at 44
I know everything on God's green earth

233
00:16:52,191 --> 00:16:56,340
about how to run a law firm and
help a client and navigate things?

234
00:16:56,360 --> 00:17:01,822
That's a fairy tale. It's not even a
fairy tale, it's just a pipe dream.

235
00:17:01,842 --> 00:17:08,130
So I think the people who are smart are
going to use this technology to gain access

236
00:17:08,150 --> 00:17:14,063
to experienced senior lawyers, bring them
into what they're working on and get

237
00:17:14,083 --> 00:17:18,493
really good advice, because
that's where the arbitrage is.

238
00:17:18,513 --> 00:17:22,323
It's not in analysis.
Analysis is a commodity now.

239
00:17:22,343 --> 00:17:27,046
Anybody can do that. Do you have the
experience and the judgment on your team?

240
00:17:27,066 --> 00:17:32,242
If you can be judgment maxing instead of
prestige maxing or credential maxing,

241
00:17:32,262 --> 00:17:37,060
then that, I think, is where you'll find
the people who'll be most successful.

242
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:43,229
[Jordan] So I feel like another hot debate
right now, when it comes to judgment in the

243
00:17:43,249 --> 00:17:47,105
legal profession, is the
fate of junior lawyers.

244
00:17:47,125 --> 00:17:53,511
And what I perceive is a little bit of an
assumption that the role of the junior

245
00:17:53,531 --> 00:17:59,826
is going to greatly diminish,
because it's just faster for a senior

246
00:17:59,846 --> 00:18:03,276
lawyer to do it themselves with AI.

247
00:18:03,296 --> 00:18:05,500
What say thee of that?

248
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:11,953
[George] I think that's making
a future prediction using

249
00:18:11,973 --> 00:18:16,680
present assumptions. And you either have
to make a future prediction using future

250
00:18:16,700 --> 00:18:20,554
assumptions, or a present prediction
using present assumptions.

251
00:18:20,574 --> 00:18:22,430
But it's the wrong mental map.

252
00:18:22,450 --> 00:18:28,911
Depending on who you read or who you
believe, lawyers right now, or access to

253
00:18:28,931 --> 00:18:34,383
legal services, is limited to only
about maybe 20%, in some cases 30,

254
00:18:34,403 --> 00:18:40,631
at most 40% of the population. I don't
know any other market where, by the use of

255
00:18:40,651 --> 00:18:44,757
technology, your total
addressable market could double.

256
00:18:44,777 --> 00:18:50,651
And so with those junior lawyers, right
now it's not cost-effective for them to

257
00:18:50,671 --> 00:18:55,101
handle a $5,000 fender bender
or a small contract dispute.

258
00:18:55,121 --> 00:18:58,523
You just settle it, walk
away, write it off.

259
00:18:58,543 --> 00:19:04,684
Well, now those types of disputes are very
cost-effective for a junior lawyer to

260
00:19:04,704 --> 00:19:10,031
handle. So I think you will still
see a lot of junior lawyers working,

261
00:19:10,051 --> 00:19:15,986
perhaps on smaller-stakes matters, because
they can do it cheaper and the stakes are

262
00:19:16,006 --> 00:19:19,970
lower, so the client is not
as upset if you screw it up.

263
00:19:19,990 --> 00:19:24,710
[George] And at the end of the day, it's kind
of like the problem we had in crypto with

264
00:19:24,730 --> 00:19:29,659
on-ramp and off-ramp. There's got to be
a human review off-ramp for this stuff.

265
00:19:29,679 --> 00:19:34,873
You can't just plug it into the machine
and go full send and let the automation go.

266
00:19:34,893 --> 00:19:38,581
At least until we replace the
court system and human nature.

267
00:19:38,601 --> 00:19:43,273
But there's got to be human review, and I
think you're just going to see more work

268
00:19:43,293 --> 00:19:46,212
that more juniors have got
to spend time reviewing.

269
00:19:46,232 --> 00:19:50,960
And if it's lower-stakes stuff, well, then
they'll be content with a junior review,

270
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:54,424
and only the higher-stakes things
should go to the senior review.

271
00:19:54,444 --> 00:19:58,676
But the dirty little secret is every other
business in the world runs like that.

272
00:19:58,696 --> 00:20:01,143
There's no reason law
firms shouldn't as well.

273
00:20:01,163 --> 00:20:05,448
And if they are, the only reason they're
doing that is to run up the bill so they

274
00:20:05,468 --> 00:20:07,310
can get their billable hours in.

275
00:20:07,330 --> 00:20:13,565
[Jordan] Right. Yeah, I think you make a really
good point that making future predictions

276
00:20:13,585 --> 00:20:17,040
with present facts is
typically ill-advised.

277
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:21,958
I think also the loudest people in the room
right now about AI are probably the ones

278
00:20:21,978 --> 00:20:26,413
who tend to have a hard time finding
ROI with delegation in the first place.

279
00:20:26,433 --> 00:20:30,290
And, you know, I'll even throw
myself under the bus with that too.

280
00:20:30,310 --> 00:20:35,150
We all know that my favorite thing to do
is just lock myself in a room for 12 hours

281
00:20:35,170 --> 00:20:38,680
with a laptop and see what kind
of magic comes out of there.

282
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:44,181
So I get it. I really do. But it's me
and everyone like me who's screaming on

283
00:20:44,201 --> 00:20:49,877
Twitter and on LinkedIn about this. It
has never been faster in the short term to

284
00:20:49,897 --> 00:20:52,553
delegate and train, for anyone. Never.

285
00:20:52,573 --> 00:20:58,180
The ROI on delegation requires an investment,
and that investment has always been

286
00:20:58,200 --> 00:21:03,807
time. And so the question is not, "Is it
faster in the short term for me to do it

287
00:21:03,827 --> 00:21:07,625
myself with AI?" Because
obviously the answer is yes.

288
00:21:07,645 --> 00:21:12,178
The question is: does AI
change the ROI equation?

289
00:21:12,198 --> 00:21:16,400
Does it make the ROI greater?
Does it keep it the same?

290
00:21:16,420 --> 00:21:18,864
Does it diminish it? Does it eliminate it?

291
00:21:18,884 --> 00:21:22,933
A lot of people think it's going to
eliminate it, but let's be honest,

292
00:21:22,953 --> 00:21:25,569
you're making that
prediction based on vibes.

293
00:21:25,589 --> 00:21:28,148
And that's fine. You're
entitled to do that.

294
00:21:28,168 --> 00:21:32,733
But I don't think that we should throw
decades of wisdom on the management of a

295
00:21:32,753 --> 00:21:38,710
professional services firm out the window
just because we have this new technology.

296
00:21:38,730 --> 00:21:42,405
[George] Yeah. I mean, a good
previous example would be runners.

297
00:21:42,425 --> 00:21:47,329
Back in the day, which I caught the
tail end of when I started my career,

298
00:21:47,349 --> 00:21:52,186
before electronic filing... and luckily
my office was in the courthouse,

299
00:21:52,206 --> 00:21:55,650
so I didn't have to go that
far to file something.

300
00:21:55,670 --> 00:21:57,080
[Jordan] What's a runner,
for people who don't know?

301
00:21:57,100 --> 00:22:02,851
[George] So you had to go physically to the
courthouse to file something, or send it by

302
00:22:02,871 --> 00:22:08,330
registered mail with multiple copies
for the clerk to stamp: one for them,

303
00:22:08,350 --> 00:22:10,629
one for the opposing
party and one for you.

304
00:22:10,649 --> 00:22:14,024
Wait for that to come back, and
then serve it on the other side.

305
00:22:14,044 --> 00:22:17,785
And then once you've got proof of
service, mail that back to the clerk.

306
00:22:17,805 --> 00:22:22,121
Well, if you wanted to do anything without
waiting two or three weeks for all that

307
00:22:22,141 --> 00:22:26,830
mail to go back and forth, you had runners,
and they were just couriers for law

308
00:22:26,850 --> 00:22:32,321
firms who would run down to the clerk,
run it back to your office, run it over to

309
00:22:32,341 --> 00:22:37,343
opposing counsel, give it to the process
server to take and go serve it on

310
00:22:37,363 --> 00:22:40,673
everybody. So you had a
courier who could do that.

311
00:22:40,693 --> 00:22:44,425
And then this magical technology
called email came along.

312
00:22:44,445 --> 00:22:49,729
And that did eliminate the runner as a
job description, and it was an entry-level

313
00:22:49,749 --> 00:22:54,827
point for a law firm. But lawyers have
not stopped hiring entry-level people.

314
00:22:54,847 --> 00:22:59,676
They're now doing different things
like intake, or managing the docket,

315
00:22:59,696 --> 00:23:04,122
or calendaring, or secretary work,
whatever, reviewing discovery.

316
00:23:04,142 --> 00:23:07,220
So that didn't go away.
The job just shifted.

317
00:23:07,240 --> 00:23:11,277
There's still just as many people working
in law firms as there were 30 years ago

318
00:23:11,297 --> 00:23:15,689
when runners were a profession. It's
just humans are doing a different task,

319
00:23:15,709 --> 00:23:18,070
because the computer handles the filing.

320
00:23:18,090 --> 00:23:23,447
[Jordan] Okay. Well, I'm running tight on time,
because I need to go argue with Claude about

321
00:23:23,467 --> 00:23:27,929
something that it's doing wrong that
I could have done faster myself.

322
00:23:27,949 --> 00:23:33,370
But before we close, what is one spicy
prediction that you have on where this is all

323
00:23:33,390 --> 00:23:37,990
headed in our profession?

324
00:23:38,010 --> 00:23:41,241
[George] That's a great question. And I hate
it when people say "that's a great question,"

325
00:23:41,261 --> 00:23:44,493
because it's just a filler to say, "I
don't know the answer and I'm trying to buy

326
00:23:44,513 --> 00:23:48,366
time to think about it." So
I'm working on not saying that.

327
00:23:48,386 --> 00:23:54,342
One spicy prediction I
have is that in the short

328
00:23:54,362 --> 00:24:00,040
term, we are going to see an explosion in

329
00:24:00,060 --> 00:24:05,806
legal demand, particularly as
a byproduct of what we call the

330
00:24:05,826 --> 00:24:12,242
slopocalypse, because lawyers,
especially unscrupulous lawyers,

331
00:24:12,262 --> 00:24:18,649
no matter what the consequences have
always been, they're going to keep sending

332
00:24:18,669 --> 00:24:21,370
in slop and trying not to get caught.

333
00:24:21,390 --> 00:24:27,323
But even worse, pro se litigants
are going to mob the court system,

334
00:24:27,343 --> 00:24:30,923
because now there's this
power-to-the-people element.

335
00:24:30,943 --> 00:24:35,923
And a good example of what was the very
frontier of this was DoNotPay.com.

336
00:24:35,943 --> 00:24:41,123
But now you can have your Claude bot go
track down the spam caller who's been

337
00:24:41,143 --> 00:24:46,323
wearing you out, get all their information,
and then launch a TCPA complaint.

338
00:24:46,343 --> 00:24:50,723
So I think the court systems are
not ready for this slopocalypse.

339
00:24:50,743 --> 00:24:55,599
They have their head in the sand, and the
judges need to take note and get ahead of

340
00:24:55,619 --> 00:25:00,952
this problem. Simple fixes could be page
limits on briefs, or asking for leave of

341
00:25:00,972 --> 00:25:06,730
the court to do more filing, raising the
cost of filing fees, which absolutely needs

342
00:25:06,750 --> 00:25:12,440
to happen. It makes no sense that a $20
million lawsuit and a $500 lawsuit have the

343
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:17,280
same filing fee. That doesn't mean
you should go crazy and be punitive,

344
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:22,834
but they are not equipped. They do not have
the staff or the resources to handle a

345
00:25:22,854 --> 00:25:27,409
flood of pro se litigation, which I think
in the short term is going to happen,

346
00:25:27,429 --> 00:25:29,700
and they need to get out ahead of it.

347
00:25:29,720 --> 00:25:33,691
[Jordan] So I'm going to give you a spicy
take that kind of dovetails on that,

348
00:25:33,711 --> 00:25:38,580
which is: people are going to stop putting
class action waivers in their contracts.

349
00:25:38,600 --> 00:25:40,580
[George] Absolutely.

350
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:45,463
[Jordan] And I have been on the

351
00:25:45,483 --> 00:25:48,180
defense side of mass arbitrations before.

352
00:25:48,200 --> 00:25:52,267
And if a plaintiff's lawyer figures out
how to do a mass action really well,

353
00:25:52,287 --> 00:25:56,565
or I guess mass individual actions is what
I mean, that's a much bigger headache

354
00:25:56,585 --> 00:25:57,840
than a class action.

355
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:03,856
[George] Well, now you can have an army of
bots, and one plaintiff's lawyer can manage 500

356
00:26:03,876 --> 00:26:09,512
lawsuits pretty easily. So the whole world
of class actions may be turning upside

357
00:26:09,532 --> 00:26:11,400
down before this is over.

358
00:26:13,310 --> 00:26:16,320
[Jordan] Cool. Well, I'm going
to go argue with Claude now.

359
00:26:16,340 --> 00:26:17,600
[George] All right. Good take.

360
00:26:17,700 --> 00:26:28,780
♪ Outro music ♪
